detail.abstract:
With the advent of the Internet of Things (IoT) and autonomous systems, as well as smart grids and industrial automation, modern telecommunication networks have begun to support more latency sensitive real time services. However, these services require stringent end-to-end delays, reliability, and availability. Such services strain the limitations of traditional centralized cloud architectures because of long backhaul paths and congestion. Consequently, the next generation of telecommunication networks have latency as the critical performance bottleneck (Elbamby et al. 2019; Hassan et al. 2018). Using a systematic analytical approach, this study examines edge computing frameworks as architectures of the deployed models, the integration of models with existing network infrastructures, and key performance models of latency, throughput, and reliability. A comparative analysis of the cloud, distributed edge, and hybrid models is used to clarify differences with respect to the real-time services provided (Hu et al. 2015; Jiang et al. 2021).&nbsp;
<div>
 The integrated results show that end user proximity data processing, prevents latency in communication, minimizes congestion in networks, and boosts service dependability. In addition, processing data at the edge improves service Quality (QoS) through response time reduction and intelligent management of system resources (Zhang et al. 2018; Premsankar et al. 2018).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 To summarize, edge computing is a technological enabler for ultra-reliable low latency communication (URLCC). Such technology is needed to sustain the current and the coming telecommunication systems that are critical and real time (Adhikari and Hazra 2022).&nbsp;
</div>
